> Does anyone know of the any VAX floating point emulation library for non-VAX
> system (esp. for PC).
> 
> I'm porting a VAX program to PC which using a single precision floating point
> in many places. Because of the FPU differences, VAX and PC give different
> rounding error. It is not negligible for the single precision for this app.
> Unfortunately I can't modify VAX program (out of my control) to use double
> precision to reduce the differences.
> 
> Speed is not issue this time, but the target is to get a same (almost) data
> without modifying the VAX program.
> 
> It is a common practices to emulate x87 FPU for the x86sx machines. I hope
> somebody has similar library for VAX floating point. Right ?
> 
> Any help, suggestion or info. would be appreciated.

        Since a VAX F_float  (single  precision  floating point) has the
    same  precision  as an IEEE Single (used by Intel  processors),  and
    since the VAX and IEEE singles have _almost_  the  same  range,  you
    shouldn't  (in  principle) have to do anything.  However, the Intel,
    especially if you have a  math  co-processor, is likely to keep more
    precision  in  the intermediate results, and I would expect that  is
    the difference you are seeing.  I believe there is a way to get your
    compiler (you didn't  say  which  language)  to  truncate  or  round
    intermediate   results   back  to  single  precision.   That  should
    reproduce the VAX results...

        -Ken
