: CITI, University of Michigan, 519 W William, Ann Arbor, MI 48103-4943

I'm not sure when RCC contributed the TOPS10 name to the monitor,
but the 4 series monitors were generally known as the "Level C" monitors
and when the 5 series monitors came out, they were known as
"Level D" monitors. TOPS-10 was more of a marketing name than anything
else; true 36-bit-heads just referred to it as "the monitor" most
of the time.

It is a matter of legend that Bob Clements' huge record collection
and his love for collecting pop records was responsible for the
TOPS-10 name. For years, there was a record collector's database
program in the DECUS library written by RCC . . .
