[I hope I got these attributions right - it was getting a bit complex #-) ]

Anyway, this discussion of origins just sent me climbing up the wall to 
my "hardcopy archive" next to the ceiling :-)

Official Digital manuals (using their spelling/capitalisation etc.) :-

1.  	decsystem10
	   users
	 handbook
	second edition

Order code:DEC-10-NGZB-D.  Copyright 1969, 1970, 1771, 1972.
Second Printing (Rev.) July 1972        [1st printing was 1971]

"The documents in this handbook reflect the following versions of the software:
	
	Monitor 5.05
	TECO version 23
	LINED version 13A
	PIP version 32

Support program version numbers are specified on page 431 of this handbook."


2.	decsystem10
	mathematical
	 languages
	  handbook
	second edition

Order code: DEC-10-KRZB-D.   Copyright 1967, 1968, 1969, 1970, 1971, 1972
7th Printing (Rev) February 1970	Update Pages May 1972


3.	decsystem10
	OPERATING SYSTEM
	COMMANDS MANUAL

	AD-0916C-T1

	March 1978

	This manual reflects the software of the 6.03 release of the monitor.

Copyright 1971, 1978
First Printing, 	  May 1971
Revised:		March 1978


Item (1) above is a sort of omnibus wherein a number of separate manuals
are bound in one volume.  The first in sequence is:

	decsystem10
	INTRODUCTION TO
	DECsystem-10 SOFTWARE

[Order code] DEC-10-MZDA-D  Copyright 1971  1st Printing November 1971

The Foreword to this section reads in part:  

"_Introduction to DECsystem-10 Software_ is not intended to be a 
programmer's reference manual.  For complete details on the operating 
system, the reader is referred to _TOPS-10 Operating System Commands_ 
(DEC-10-MRDA-D) and _TOPS-10 Monitor Calls_ (DEC-10-MRRA-D)."


One of the things I found quite intriguing is that CHAPTER 1 / INTRODUCTION
of item (3) above [printed March 1978] leads out with the statement:

"The DECsystem-10 Operating System, called TOPS-10, ... "

yet on the back of the title page, along with copyright notices etc., is
the statement:

"The following are trademarks of Digital Equipment Corporation: "

which is followed by a list of 26 items, including DIGITAL, DEC, PDP,
DECsystem-10, DECsystem-20, DECtape ... but *no* TOPS-10  :-)

Similarly, _decsystem10 / FORTRAN PROGRAMMER'S / REFERENCE MANUAL_
(Order No. AA-0944E-TB) for FORTRAN-10, Version 5, which was printed
in January 1977, does not include "TOPS-10" in the trademark listing;
but it does include TYPESET-10, which was not included in the later 
printing of (3) though both manuals include -8 and -11.  So someone
was doing a bit of editing, but "TOPS-10" still didn't make it as a
trademark at that time.

Cheers,  Ian S.

P.S.  As a matter of peripheral interest, the earlier manuals (1 & 2 above)
both list only six trademarks:  DEC, FLIP CHIP, DIGITAL, PDP, FOCAL, 
and COMPUTER LAB - all of which are included in the 26/27 listed in later docs
(by then one had multiplied too:  COMPUTER LAB had become COMPUTER LABS :):):)


