>In the case of OSI, I think they're going to get a resounding "no
>thanks". OSI is dead, and only DEC seems to think otherwise. (There are
>certain technologies from OSI that aren't, but OSI is dead as a protocol
>suite except at DEC sites.) I hope TGV, TWG, etc. are all prepared to
>handle the additional volume of orders for their products. :-)

Sorry, but I believe you're wrong there. OSI is not dead. In fact, as 
companies become more familiar with all aspects of Decnet/OSI, more 
and more are opting for the upgrade. In Canada for instance, companies are 
moving to OSI in record numbers. Not necessarily Decnet/OSI, but an 
OSI stack in general. If OSI were dead, then why would vendors such 
as Sun, IBM, etc. be marketing their  own versions of OSI?
Companies are looking for a standard that's not going to be obsolete in 5 
years, like TCP/IP. As for IPNG? Forget it. Read the conferences.

As for TGV, why would they even start a  Decnet-like product if TCP/IP were 
going to take over the world like they imply in all their ads? The answer is 
simple, companies that currently use Decnet like it.

Before you write off OSI, look into the facts. Don't listen to the hype from 
other vendors that are trying to push their own products. Digital is not 
"putting you back against the wall" and Decnet/OSI is not a "nightmare" as 
some would have you believe.

Before you opt for completely changing your network environment, check the 
record first.

BTW:
I've been working with OSI from the beginning. I've also worked with IP for 
years. I like both. I see a place for both, depending on what the needs are.


