In article <jimh.24.000D60E8@sosi.com>, jimh@sosi.com (Jim Hodges) writes:
> Before you write off OSI, look into the facts. Don't listen to the hype from 
> other vendors that are trying to push their own products.

Good idea! Let's have a few facts:

1. Most OSI products still implement X.400(84).

2. OSI products that label themselves as "new" implement X.400(88).

3. X.400(92) has been out for 2 years. I have yet to hear of a product that
   implements it.

4. The OSI review/ballot process is very slow. This causes the standards to be
   based on the technology that was the state of the art about 2 years before.

Conclusions:

A. Most X.400 products implement a protocol that was released 10 years ago and
   that really corresponds to 1982 technology.

B. The more recent products implement 1986 technology.

In addition, I can tell you that X.400 is one of the top causes of frustration
for gateway/mailing-list administrators. X.400 gateways are buggy, the error
messages are usually sent to the wrong place, and they are unintelligible. Not
that I think "unknown imail error -278238484" or "not a typewriter" are very
meaningful, mind you, but at least one can blame these errors on unix and/or a
bug in the mail program. The X.400 delivery errors, on the other hand, are not
due to a broken system configuration file or to a system error. They were meant
to be as they are - totally unintelligible.

I have been exposed to X.400 on a daily basis since 1985 (well, maybe not daily
in 1985). In 1985, EARN's politicians had decided to migrate to OSI by the end
of the year. Things dragged on. The software didn't work, and nobody wanted it.
DEC opened a dedicated Operations Centre in Amsterdam with a staff of 5-6 just
to help us migrate to OSI. Things dragged on again. Users didn't want X.400,
system administrators were not happy with the software, network administrators
were not happy with the protocol efficiency and with the gateways. They were
broken in 1985, they are still broken 9 years later. The migration was more or
less cancelled in 1987-8 (it was never officially stated, people just stopped
working on it and politicians stopped whipping them or even talking about OSI,
while DEC closed the operation centre and the person who had pushed this idea
within DEC was "rewarded" for the staggering success). Maybe one day ISO will
finally accept that X.400 is dead and that the world is better off without it.
Maybe.

  Eric


