In article <id.4_DC1.4F4@nmti.com>, peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) writes...
>In article <23AUG199421165602@tgv.com>,
>Kenneth Adelman <adelman@tgv.com> wrote:
>>     CTERM over TCP/IP. (Which, btw, has some advantages over TELNET
>> on long-haul lines, like better interactive response since the line
>> editing, etc, is now local.
>
>How does that differ from telnet LINEMODE?

    Do you mean RFC1184 linemode or the "defacto" linemode that occurs
when remote echo suppression is not enabled?  As far as I know, there
is no server implementation of RFC1184 linemode, and the VMS terminal
driver doesn't provide all of the interfaces you need to properly
implement one.	CTERM, however, is basically the same idea as RFC1184
linemode, only VMS-specific in ways that let it work against the VMS
terminal driver and more efficiently under VMS.

    If a client and server implementation of RFC1184 existed for VMS,
it would perform almost as good as CTERM.

							Ken


