>Wouldn't it be something if one of the 3rd party DECnet vendors were to
>step in to the void with a full-function DECnet phase IV product for VMS
>6.2+. :-)

No need, really. Take a look at Phase/IP from TGV. It provides the DECnet API
over TCP/IP. No static node name files (NETNODE_REMOTE). Since it uses BIND,
there is no need for DECdns, either. In fact, if all the nodes you deal with
are running Phase/IP, there is no need for DECnet. I've been able to run all of
the "standard" DECnet apps quite transparently. Set Host a.yoyo.com:: is weird
looking, but very nice. And there is a noticable improvement in performance.

If you have both DECnet and TCP, it will use both with the preference set in
the configuration. If you are running VMS prior to 6.1 (VAX) or 1.5 (AXP), it
had mapping capacity to map 6 character node names to full domain names.

Full DECnet functionality with none of the hassle.

I think this is starting to read like an ad, so I'll quit here. I have no ties
to TGV. I just think this is the neatest product to come down the pike in a
long time.

