writes:
>In article <Cus3t9.EuC@apollo.hp.com>,
>Rick Schofield <rds@ch.apollo.hp.com> wrote:
>>
>>
>>If the drive has been powered off for longer than 2-3 minutes, when I boot
>>the Adaptec BIOS finds and maps both devices (the working RZ24L at device 0
>>as C and the RZ26 at device 1 as D).  However, any accesses to the RZ26 from
>>that point on result in a drive fault (led on drive goes amber and red led on
>>the 1542B lights up).  Rebooting does not clear the fault condition - only
>>powering off for 2-3 minutes.
>	This sounds like you may have the old ROM on the 154x which can't
>	handle over 1GB.  Maybe someone remembers the required version?
>
>>
>>>Are you positive that you have a -E. The first one that I got was a -B.
>>>The -B will NOT work on a PC.
>>>
>>I didn't know this.  What's the difference and why doesn't the -B work in
>>PC's?
>>
>	The RZ26s I know of are: RZ26 (made by DEC, aka DSP3105 with the
>	generic OEM firmware), RZ26B (rebadged HP drive), and RZ26L (low
>	profile, not sure of manufacturer but I believe Digital.)  When
>	ordering RZ26s from Digital there are letter AFTER the - which
>	mainly control mounting brackets, factory/field installation, and
>	jumper/mode page setting.  Never heard of RZ26E.  The RZ26B was
>	indeed sold on DECpcs.
If the drive has a fault LED, it should be an RZ26 (like DSP3105, 1.6" high)
or an RZ26L (1" high, like DSP3107L). The RZ26B is indeed an HP buyout and it
doesn't have a fault LED (as far as I know.) There should be part number and
serial number labels on the drive, if you could describe a little more what is
on the stickers (I didn't get the full thread)?

There are several more different part numbers (like RZ26E etc.) which describe
special variants, but they all consist of an RZ26, RZ26B or RZ26L with options,
e.g. jumpers, mounting kits, mode page settings etc. like Sam mentions.

If a customer tells me the fault LED comes on, I start with the assumption that
the drive is faulty. This only happens if (applies to internal drives only,
not the HP drives which I don't know)
1. The CPU doesn't come up (hardware default state is on)
2. It doesn't complete its POST
3. The firmware detects a fatal drive malfunction (dead head, spinup problem,
   servo doesn't calibrate etc.)
4. It is turned on by a MODE SELECT.
Number 4 doesn't happen unless you have a system that is very special, so it
must be one of the other cases. The thermal dependency you describes makes it
seem that the drive fails. Since the drive spins up, case 3 seems applicable.
 
The 1 GB limitation on certain Adaptecs can not cause the fault LED to light.

Hope this helps (I'm afraid it's the opposite)
Ralf-Peter
