In article <33955b$lst@cumulus.CAM.ORG>, jean@CAM.ORG (Jean Laganiere) writes:
>
>I bought a uVAX II for very cheap and I am trying to install
>ULTRIX version 4.4 on it.
>
>I boot with the TK50 using the ULTRIX boot/upgrade tape.
>(All the control buttons are in the out position)
>Then I chose the BASIC Installation from the menu.
>After the SYSTEM DISK SELECTION (RD54) I get this message:
>
>   The installation procedure now allocate the root file system on
>   partition 'a' of the system disk, ra0 RD54.
>
>   Making the root file system on ra0 RD54.
>   wtfs: Not a typewriter

"Not a typewriter" is usually the message associated with a
failed I/O control on the device.  That the system recognized
it as an RD54 suggests that it is formatted correctly.  Does
the M7555 take a full Q-bus slot or half a slot?

>
>1- Is that realy mean that my RD54 is not usable anymore?

Sounds like something is broken, but I'm not sure what.

>3- Do I have to do a low level format before, and how?

You need either the customer dianostic tape or the field
service dialognostic tape.  If it wasn't included in what
you bought, go back and see if the vendor/seller has one.

>6- Can I install the two main board in the same machine, since ULTRIX
>   support multiprocessor?

Nope.  While ULTRIX does support SMP on the right systems, the
MicroVAX II isn't one them.  Use that slot for the memory
instead.

