prep@yarrow.wt.uwa.edu.au (Paul Repacholi) writes:

>In article <CuxqD4.4s6@qdpii.ind.dpi.qld.gov.au> ianst@qdpii.ind.dpi.qld.gov.au (Ian Staples) writes:
>> Actually, I have very fond memories of the pdp10.  I spent most of 1973
>> using one interactively [interactively?  with a tty: ? :) ] at the Univ. of
>> Queensland in Brisbane. 

>Was that TOPS-20 or bottoms? One of the opers used the account name
>of Zaphod. You didn't need completion for the next bit ;-)

Actually, when I first started using the beast the O/S was the Series 4
Monitor which had been *seriously* hacked by the local computer science
crowd to customise it.  This seemed to mean removing some useful features
and largely destroying the stability of the system, probably in the interests
of a local accounting system.  One got used to saving work every 10 minutes.
It was said that people who knew what they were doing would crash the thing
at the end of every big job because you didn't get billed for resources if
you were still logged in when it died.

Midway through 1973 they introduced the 5 Series Monitor (hope I'm 
remembering these names correctly) which they didn't have time to stuff
around with and as a result things were *much* better after that :-)
For an introductory period we were allowed free use at weekends to practise
on the new system and, thanks to DECtapes and a friendly operator, I was
able to do a fair bit of useful work for nix and transfer the output to
the production system when 5 replaced 4.

A most useful feature which wasn't disabled in 5 was the ability to write
to other directories.  With a bit of preparation you could copy large
files to another area and so avoid the logout limits on disk space :):):)

Later (around 1974-76 I guess) I continued some of the work sporadically
on the PDP-10 at James Cook University in Townsville.  I think Tops-10
may have come in around then - or maybe it was after I'd really finished
what I had to do (I seem to recall enquiring about manuals, but I'm not
sure if I ever actually used Tops-10 [or later?] systems - except very
briefly when recovering stuff from archive tapes to floppy disks when
the demise of the PDP-10 was imminent at JCU a few years ago).

One of the biggest impressions I had at the time had little to do with
computers and a lot to do with "efficiency" and "economies of scale".
At UofQ, a *large* University, things were very autocratic.  The system
shut down every midnight and didn't start again till 0800; holidays were
a dead loss, etc. etc.  At JCU, then a *small* University, things were
very democratic.  The system was accessible at all hours and was simply
left running during holidays etc.  (Bad luck if a lightning strike or
something took it down, but even then there was a good chance one of the
operators would be sufficiently public-spirited to come back from the
beach and turn it on again :)

Cheers,  Ian S.

