> (James Williams) writes...
> 
>>Actually most VAXen won't run without a PDP-11.  78x use an LSI-11 as console,
>>86x0 use a weird T-11 based system, and several (unknown to me) use a PRO-380
> 
> Um, I think I'd quibble with the term "most".  If memory serves me
> correctly, the only models with the PDP-11 are the ones that you cited
> (78x and 86x0) plus 9xxx; and the following models do not contain an
> embedded pdp-11:
> 
>     VAX 8530, 8550			  
>     VAX 8700, 8800, 8810, 8820, 8830, 8840
>     VAX 8842, 8974, 8978

The Nautilus prcessors used a DEC Pro as their console device, with
rather ugly (and slow) comms between the Pro, the processor and the 
environment monitor module.  

Note that the 897x is just a package based around a cluster of 8700s.
However, a single MicroVAX II usually replaced the Pro 380s that would 
have come with individual 8700s.

The 8550, 8700 & 8800 were the same processor; the 8700 & 8800 were a 
two-processor box; the 8800 has both processors installed whereas the 
8700 is upgradable to an 8800 by adding a second processor.

The 8500 & 8530 are a cut-down Nautilus; the 8530 is identical to an 8500 
except that the microcode has a few NOPs taken out.  The 8500->8530 
upgrade comes on floppies (or in the case of the machine I worked on, 
on a fresh RD52 installed into the console Pro).  8530->8550 upgrade 
involves (among other things) a backplane swap, so it's not quite so 
trivial...

The 85xx used a Pro 350.  I believe the 8700 & 8800 used a Pro 380.

The PolarStar (88x0, where x > 0) could take 4 (modified?) Nautilus 
processors.  I can't remember what this brute used as a console.

> The embedded PDP-11 on the VAX 11/780 was fun, though.  While VMS was up
> you could dismount the console floppy and use the drive for other
> purposes, for example to write a floppy that you wanted to carry over to
> the RSX system down the hall.  Just better not forget to put the console
> floppy back in, or the next auto-reboot wouldn't work.  I think
> switching floppies around was also how we instructed our 11/782 whether
> to come up as an 11/782 or as two 11/780's.  

The Pros on the Nautiluses (Nautili?) would run quite independently of
the VAX.  You could exit from the console program and scrounge around the
Pro's hard disk without any trouble at all.  The console program let you
mount the hard disk from the VAX as well (read-only -- trying to mount it
for write would crash the system in at least one version of the console
software, which was always a bug farm -- I think DEC had just about got
it to work around release 10), and I reverse-engineered the console log
format, and had a little program that would grab the last 200 or so lines
of the log off the console disk as the machine completed system startup
so that we could check for bugs in the startup procedures without needing
a console printer.  (The program could be run manually too if we
suspected there were console log messages that were nowhere else.) 


