I missed the original post but here is our experiences of the original 6Gbyte
Cypher DLT with 7 cartridge autochanger

I believe the current higher capacity drives are faster

Real world backup speeds from last weeks backup log using 
dump 0uf /dev/nrmt2h /dev/rrz?c of local disks on Decstation 5000/260

Backup size   throughput
Kbytes        Kbytes/Min
860853 		36069
935618 		27776
438633 		25354
851845 		35993
625859 		38593
22606 		33909

On using rsh remote_host dump 0uf - /dev/rrz?c | dd of=/dev/nrm2h obs=10240
the throughput depends on the cpu power of remote end 25-35Mbyte/min on
5000/240 & Alpha's down to about 10Mbytes/min on Decstation 2100

Although it was dead on arrival and had to be replaced it has been 
100% reliable since then ( over 1 year)

However when the tape has a lot of small incremental dumps on it a command 
like 
mt -f /dev/nrmt2h fsf 40
will only skip about 30 to 35 files.
Issuing mt -f /dev/nrmt2h fsf 1    
40 times does skip 40 files.

One word of warning - a college has a identical drive connected to a SUN and
he complains that it is slower than his 4mm drive - It does not worry him so
has not looked into the reason. 

Hope this helps.
