>In article <32qb35$l4@raven.dow.com>, jsue@dow.com (Jeffrey L. Sue) writes:
>> [other] Vendors also drop applications software for new ones, often
>> with no upgrade plan in place (ie., you're stuck & have to buy
>> all over again).
>> 
>> Why is Digital being lambasted for doing the same.

Well, for one thing, many of us are long term DEC customers who don't
have a great deal of experience with other vendors. DEC hasn't done this
in the past, and now they're doing it wholesale. We're not used to being
treated this way, and it ticks us off. The fact that we might have been
treated this way all along by another vendor is irrelevant to our
perception of the problem, because we haven't been in that situation. I
imagine people who've always worked at mixed sites and have had this
happen to them are less upset.

>> The major difference to me is that Digital is forcing all these
>> issues at once

But that's a biggie as well. Every trick they pull costs us money. Doing
a lot of it at once costs a lot of money in a short time. 

For instance, I must now decide whether to never upgrade past VMS 6.1,
buy enough Multinet licenses to cover those of my machines that don't
run it, or by OSI routers, because DEC has decided that they won't do
host based routing for OSI, and they're dropping DECnet IV. None of
these options is appealing, and none is cheap (freezing the version
isn't really an option, unless I commit to dumping VMS in a specific
time frame, for reasons of remaining supported).

IMHO, if DEC kills a software product, they should distribute the source
freely so that if anyone wants to, they can support it themselves. I'm
not saying I'd run DECnet IV without support, but I might. But I'll bet
there are people on the net who'd pick up maintenance of VAX SCAN, for
instance, and maybe even port it to AXP.
