	You can't format your RZ55 because you haven't spun it up yet.
The RZ55 must have the spin up on power up bit set or it will not spin
up when power is applied. It will also not allow you to format or mount
it (which makes sense) until the drive is spun up. Drive 7 from Casa
Blanca Works, FWB HD Toolkit, and La Cie's Silverlining should all be 
able to spin up the drive, and I believe that they each allow you to 
access the drive's parameter RAM to set the bit permenantly. This bit
can also be set if you move the drive back to a VAXstation and use test
73;

>>>T 73
KA42-A TPmker

VStmk_QUE_id (0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7) ? 0 <----Enter ID address of drive
VStmk_QUE_RUsure (1/0) ? <----Enter 42000001 ( to ENABLE spin up bit
                         <----Enter 41000001 ( to DISABLE spin up bit

	The test complains of failures, but that's OK it's actually a 
test for tape devices.

	BTW, before purchasing any of the formatters I mentioned above
please check with the vendor to see if they can actually do what I claim
they so. I have heard from others that they have had success getting 
the drive to spinup with FWB, and Drive 7, I have also heard that FWB 
and Silverlining have allowed some to set the bit but no one has sent
me exact instructions on how to get these programs to set the bit or
to spin up the drive. 

	One other note, older Macs (pre Mac SE) will not be able to boot
from the RZ55. I'm not sure why, but it's something in the Mac ROMs not
the RZ55 drive that's at fault.

	The reason Digital chose not to allow the drive to spin up on
power up is that we typically see several SCSI disks in our system 
configurations. Allowing all the disks to spin up when power was 
applied would certainly trip breakers or blow fuses in our system or
expansion boxes. Instead we choose to allow the operating system to 
issue a spin up command to the device when it is mounted.
	 
