>
>>#2- i'm running OS2 2.1. However, I was using windoze 3.1 when I first
>>   got the drive.
>
>I have both on my existing RZ24L.  In fact, although I'm using mostly DOS
>at the moment, I'm booting thru the OS/2 boot manager.  I think I tried using
>OS/2 to format and partition the drive once but had problems.  Although 
>thinking back now, it's possible I had the termination in the middle of the
>bus instead of at the end...
	First thing to always check is termination and/or cables.

>
>>#3- i believe i was using aspi2dos when I was running windoze.
>>
>
>If you are using a 1422, then this should be the right one.  I'm told that
>for adaptec xx2x cards, you use ASPI2DOS.SYS and for xx4x cards you use
>ASPI4DOS.SYS.  Do you know if this is true?
	Again, 1422 doesn't sound right.  152x uses ASPI2DOS, 154x uses
	ASPI4DOS, 174x (in enhanced mode only) uses ASPIEDOS.

>
>>what happens when your system boots? You should get a scsi message with
>>lun addresses. Make sure the drive is spinning. Try taking the
>>terminator off of the drive.
>
>If the drive has been powered off for longer than 2-3 minutes, when I boot
>the Adaptec BIOS finds and maps both devices (the working RZ24L at device 0
>as C and the RZ26 at device 1 as D).  However, any accesses to the RZ26 from
>that point on result in a drive fault (led on drive goes amber and red led on
>the 1542B lights up).  Rebooting does not clear the fault condition - only
>powering off for 2-3 minutes.
	This sounds like you may have the old ROM on the 154x which can't
	handle over 1GB.  Maybe someone remembers the required version?

>
>>Are you positive that you have a -E. The first one that I got was a -B.
>>The -B will NOT work on a PC.
>>
>I didn't know this.  What's the difference and why doesn't the -B work in
>PC's?
>
	The RZ26s I know of are: RZ26 (made by DEC, aka DSP3105 with the
	generic OEM firmware), RZ26B (rebadged HP drive), and RZ26L (low
	profile, not sure of manufacturer but I believe Digital.)  When
	ordering RZ26s from Digital there are letter AFTER the - which
	mainly control mounting brackets, factory/field installation, and
	jumper/mode page setting.  Never heard of RZ26E.  The RZ26B was
	indeed sold on DECpcs.

