kolling@adobe.com (Karen Kolling) writes:

>In article <1994Aug8.103239.1010@rhodes> stuart@zen.mathcs.rhodes.edu (Brian L. Stuart) writes:
>>.....
>>5)  The K?10 and KL20 processors were used in the DECSystem10 and DECSystem20
>>    models and were sometimes referred to as the PDP-10.  This is one
>>    area of DEC lore that I'm not real clear on.  Could someone help
>>    add the details here?

>Originally there was the PDP-10, and its processors were the KA, then
>the KI, then the KL.

KA10, I believe because it was the first -10 processor.  (The first memory
was the MA10.)  KI10 because it was the first -10 processor to be built with
Integrated circuits.  KL because it was the first -10 processor built
with ECL, and L was the first letter in ECL that comes after I.  Also,
don't forget the KS10, which was a Small -10.  The KA and KI had lotsa
lights, the KL and KS were not nearly so endearing.

This is mostly from memory and most assuredly wrong:
KA10	1968
KI10	1972
KL10	1976
KS10	1978

> Around about the time of
>the KL, maybe a bit earlier, someone at Dec decided to rename the
>PDP-10s DECsystem-10s

Earlier, close to the KI10 timeframe.  The name change happened with the
switch from PDP-10 monitor 4S72 (Version 4, swapping support, edit 72)
to 5.01 (major changes, new file system greater interest in competing in
the commercial marketplace).

  Also roughly about this time,
>BB&N's TENEX operating system for the (unmodified, I think...) PDP-10
>was brought in house by Dec and turned into TOPS-20.

Tenex ran on a KA10 with a pager box designed and built by BB&N.  At DEC
it was first ported to the KI10 with a lot of software emulation for the
BB&N pager.  The KL pager was designed to let Tenex/TOPS-20 run well.  The
pure KA10 had some segmentation hardware that was used to give programs code
and data sections. (Hiseg and loseg.)  The hisegs were sharable among the
various users.

>Karen Kolling
>former supervisor, PDP-10 Interface group
>(ah, JFFO, JFCL, sniff, sniff...)

My favorites were AOBJN and ILDB.


