In article <1994Aug14.082827.7452@ivax>, imhw400@indyvax.iupui.edu (Mark H. Wood) writes:
> Jeez, I think there must be a disgruntled former KGB disinformation specialist
> out there who makes this stuff up to keep in practice.  "Digital to Abandon
> 100,000 Paying Customers" -- gimme a break.
> 
> Is it just me, or am I beginning to see a sinister pattern to these "rumors"?

  Well, the folks at DEC seem to concentrate on putting out press releases
about the latest shuffling of VP's, and rarely mention any sort of commit-
ment to VMS (or anything else for that matter).

  Between that, the layoffs (which are painfully obvious to anyone who has
DEC hardware or software service, or who's trying to buy same), the stock
price, and the fact that DEC *has* jettisoned whole product lines in the
past (consider the DECsystem-10/20, and more recently the PDP-11 product line
and the disk drive business), I can see how these things get started.

  On the other hand, VMS has got to be pretty strategic to DEC - after all,
it's one of the few things keeping their customers from running on Unix boxes
(or PC's, or whatever the platform du jour is). The question is, does DEC
*understand* that VMS is strategic? Good question - sometimes they don't act
like it.

  All that aside, I can't see DEC dropping VMS (or vending it out like the
PDP-11's and disks). I _could_ envision VAX support (as opposed to AXP's/
Alpha's/Pepsi Generation/whatever the name is this week) being phased out in
a few years - for a number of reasons: 1) Many VAX shops are comfortably
parked at VMS V5.5-2 or below and have no intention of upgrading (and that
means no intention of buying new hardware, all of which wants V5.5-2H4 or
V6.something) 2) I expect DEC's hardware development efforts to be concen-
traded on AXP processors, with only another generation or two of VAX chips.
3) Some new features are only going to show up on AXP (like "The New File
System"). Thus, I expect customers to settle into two groups - the ones that
are happy with what they have, VAX-wise, and thus won't upgrade, and those
that see the writing on the wall and convert to AXP's (or some other vendor's
platform).

