In article <32m955$lvd@agate.berkeley.edu>, sf@luther.ced.berkeley.edu (Susan Feng) writes:
|>
|>Greetings,
|>
|>I connected a RRD42 CDROM an alpha DEC300 machine in order to install 
|>some software subsets. The installation doc told me I can find RRD42 
|>device name by "file /dev/rrz*c", however, my
|>system doesn't show RRD42 device:
|>
	[ stuff deleted ]
|>
|>What do I need to do to let the system recognize RRD42?
|>
|>Thanks a lot,
|>
|>XueShan
|>--

Before you try to reconfigure a kernel, try this first:

cd /dev
./MAKEDEV rz4

This will make the special files for the CDROM.  Then try the "file" command
again.  This should take care of it.  This assumes you are installing on SCSI
bus 0.  If you install on bus 1, then change rz4 to rz12.

Should that fail, you can eadd the appropriate line to the config file
(referenced in another reply) and rebuild and reboot the new kernel.  If you
didn't customize your config file, don't use the -c flag of doconfig.  This will
cause a new configuration file to be created for your system.  If the CDROM 
drive is connected and ON when you do this, the entry for it will automatically
be added to the config file.

Dave


