In article <32o1rm$8rb@transfer.stratus.com>, jamesw@sw.stratus.com 
(James Williams) writes...

>Actually most VAXen won't run without a PDP-11.  78x use an LSI-11 as console,
>86x0 use a weird T-11 based system, and several (unknown to me) use a PRO-380

Um, I think I'd quibble with the term "most".  If memory serves me
correctly, the only models with the PDP-11 are the ones that you cited
(78x and 86x0) plus 9xxx; and the following models do not contain an
embedded pdp-11:

    VAXft3000 Model 310		 
    MicroVAX I, II			 
    MicroVAX 2000
    MicroVAX 3100 (Model 10, 20, 10e, 20e, 30, 40, 80, 90 ...)
    MicroVAX 3300, 3400, 3500, 3600, 3800, 3900		 
    VAXstation II		 
    VAXstation 2000		 
    VAXstation 3100 (Model 30, 40, 38, 48, 76...)
    VAXstation 3200, 3500, 3520, 3540
    VAXstation 4000 VLC, Model 60, ...
    VAXstation 8000		  
    VAX 11/725, 11/730, 11/750
    VAX 4000 Model 200, 300    
    VAX 6000 Model 2x0, 3x0, 4x0, 5x0, 312, 333, 423, 424 
    VAX 7000-6x0, 7x0      		 
    VAX 8200, 8250
    VAX 8300, 8350
    VAX 8530, 8550			  
    VAX 8700, 8800, 8810, 8820, 8830, 8840
    VAX 8842, 8974, 8978

There are other VAXes than the above, which also do not have a PDP-11
included.  The above was taken from a handy list of models I kept for
myself until several years ago. 

The embedded PDP-11 on the VAX 11/780 was fun, though.  While VMS was up
you could dismount the console floppy and use the drive for other
purposes, for example to write a floppy that you wanted to carry over to
the RSX system down the hall.  Just better not forget to put the console
floppy back in, or the next auto-reboot wouldn't work.  I think
switching floppies around was also how we instructed our 11/782 whether
to come up as an 11/782 or as two 11/780's.  

Trivia question: why did we choose the names RAND:: and MCNALY:: for the
two nodes of our 11/782?  (Of course, when operating in 11/782 mode only
one of the names was used.)

        /john


