In article <1994Aug17.164537@kuttner.sfc.sony.com> Eric_Weaver@avtc.sel.sony.com writes:
>
>What was the name of the call that returned a generator for wildcard
>filenames?  I recall that one used GNJFN on the result of it.

Plain old GTJFN (with the GJ%IFG flag set to return an "indexable" file
handle).  GNJFN "steps" the JFN to the next file.

	Background:

	JSYS (Jump to SYStem) is the opcode used for system calls.

	On TOPS-20, the "open()" operation is two steps; GTJFN
	returns a "file handle" (Job File Number).  OPENF opens
	the file referenced by the JFN. Some operations take unopended
	JFNs (ie; RNAMF for renaming).  JFNs can always be translated
	back to strings using the JFNS JSYS.

There is also a WILD JSYS, but it just compares two strings using
wildcarding.

-Phil Budne
