In article <id.KK4C1.BY1@nmti.com>, Peter da Silva <peter@nmti.com> wrote:
>I realise this might be a heretical suggestion, but I suspect that someone
>up in ai.mit.edu would be able to lay their hands on a copy of ITS. It wouldn't
>be TOPS, but it would be more interesting than anything else that's likely
>to be widely available...
>
>Someone mentioned a -20 emulator in another message. Has anyone got ITS up on
>that?

Yes, ancient history.  Actually I believe ITS is the *first *OS that Ken
Harrenstien got his KLH10 working with.  But last I heard he still wouldn't
distribute KLH10, no idea why not (claims that it would be distributed
under the GPL by 1.5 years ago appear to have been exaggerated).

ITS is available in all its glory (sources, executables, and snapshots of
user accounts) for anonymous FTP from mc.lcs.mit.edu.  There's been some
recent discussion on the ITS mailing list about whether this is 100% OK,
but the consensus was that whether or not MIT *can* sue you for helping
yourself to ITS, there's no reason why they ever would.  Whether hearsay
assurances like that are good enough for you (and your company) to take
the plunge and go for it is up to you...

Anyway the one thing that you can't get out of the ITS snapshots is the
tape boot block (it's nestled inside the program that writes it, which
you need a working ITS to run...).  I have it, along with programs to
turn the UNIX files from the FTP server back into 36-bit tape files
(TM03 byte packing), if anyone wants either let me know.

Also if anyone has an actual, real need for particular KS10 parts, ask
me.  I'd rather see them in your working machine than in my storage
locker, but I'd rather see them in my storage locker than on your coffee
table or hung on your office wall.  I have no spare CSL boards and
very few UBAs, but a decent amount of everything else (and racks, BA11Ks,
and CPU boxes galore, as long as you don't mind brown paint -- they're
from ADP).  No payment required as long as you successfully fix your
machine (I sure wouldn't mind an account!), I just don't want to give
my rare stuff to dreamers.

John Wilson    JOHNW@ZP (Pinhead ITS, on ice until machine room is done)


