In article <32pcgd$4j6@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, bschor@vms.cis.pitt.edu writes:
>      Two or so years ago, DN&R had a front page cover story "DEC to Drop 
> PDP-11 Line".  It wasn't true then (DEC had just announced the /93 and 
> /94), and I don't believe it is true now (but am prepared to be educated).  
> What DEC "jettisoned" was PDP-11 Software Support, which has been given to
> Mentec, Inc. (who seem to be quite interested in doing a decent job of it).

  You are correct in that what was transfered to Mentec was PDP-11 software
support. The currently-marketed PDP-11 processors (11/93 and 11/94) are built
by another company (Rolf Obler Industrieelektronik) and integrated into DEC
cabinets with other peripherals which are mostly common with older MicroVAX
systems (the non-S-box cards). If and when DEC discontinues non-S-box Q-bus
controllers, you can expect the PDP-11 product line to either be transfered
to another vendor or discontinued. Oh, and by the way Mentec hasn't responded
to either paper mail or email regarding the transfer, so I wouldn't say that
they're "quite interested"...

  However, it is true that DEC is getting out of various lines of business
when they decide that a) it isn't profitable, or b) it isn't central to their
business operation. This is happening both at the corporate level (where we
can add Digital Press, DRS, and various layered products to my earlier list)
as well as at the local/regional level. One state's Field Service group sent
a letter to all customers saying the would no longer quote service contracts
on MicroVAX III or older systems.

  All of this combines to create an atmosphere of "will the DEC products I
use that are critical to my business be cut next?", which is very similar to
the "will I be cut next?" worries that Digital employees have. This environ-
ment fuels the rumors about VMS, Rdb, and DEC itself - after all, if DEC could
cut X, why not Y?

