Likewise for alt.sys.perq; when I started alt.sys.perq, I wasn't sure
if it would sink or swim, as there aren't exactly a lot of PERQ owners
these days.  I was hoping that it would do at least as well as the
PERQ mailing list, and it did.  A relatively small number of people
who still own PERQs, and are quite dedicated to using and preserving
their PERQs, have made the newsgroup a success.  Hence, a relatively
small number (like less than 20 or 30) of dedicated people who are
very interested in a machine can make the newsgroup successful and
will find the newsgroup to be very useful.

Such individual newsgroups are necessary since people interested in a
specific machine can discuss a specific machine without having to read
through a lot of non-pertinent messages (except for a few random
irritating commercial messages crossposted to all newsgroups by some
marketroid trying to get rich quick by advertising on the net at our
expense).  It also makes it easier to archive information about such
machines.

