>ever written. PC-Zork stinks...)

The original ADVENT (remember, 6 character file names) was written
to make the use of some of the more interesting parts of the architecture.  
First off, instead of the usual compile / load / save sequence, you'd
compile the program, load it (the linking step, but the final
program is in memory, not on disk) and run, skipping the save.
ADVENT would read the data file and build all of the initial data 
structures, then "pause". In TOPS-10, this drops you back to monitor 
level. Then you save the core image (.sav). This is the file that you 
usually run and saves all of the cpu cycles of loading the data
for every run.  You could also save the game by ^c'ing and saving
the core image.

It also used 5 ascii characters packed into a word (and wasted a bit).
A friend ported it to a 3033 once, he had to resort to SIXBIT to get
the character packing to work.

You DO remember SIXBIT, don't you?  :-)

