In article <32dtpa$te@elna.ethz.ch>, system@debye.vmsmail.ethz.ch 
(Ulrich Schmitt, Phys. Chem., ETH Zurich) writes:
|In a ``DELNI Connected Configuration'', i.e., AUI of the DELNI
|(port multiplier or multiport transceiver) connected to ThickWire
|Ethernet cable by means of a transceiver (e.g., DEC H4005 or
|Lantronix LTR-5) should the heartbeat switch of the external
|transceiver be set to enabled or disabled ?
|
|I was told the DELNI should be treated like a multiport repeater
|in this respect, i.e., heartbeat disabled, but then
|NCP> SHOW KNOWN LINE COUNTER  reports many  Collision detect check failures
|(that is on the VAX/VMS maschines connected to the ports of the DELNI).

The DELNI propagates heartbeat from the global AUI port onto its
8 transceiver ports.  Therefore, if you have DTEs (computers,
terminal servers, routers, bridges) on the transceiver ports,
you should have heartbeat turned ON on the external transceiver.
However, if you have repeaters plugged into the transceiver ports
(which is not recommended in any case), you MUST have heartbeat
switched OFF.

By the way, if the global port is switched into LOCAL mode, then the
DELNI will heartbeat on all transceiver ports.  If you attach
an AUI loopback plug on the global port and switch it into GLOBAL,
that will cause the heartbeat to go away.

Now: what if you have a MIX of repeaters and DTEs attached to
your DELNI?  Again, this is not recommended; however, given that
repeaters will NOT WORK tolerably if their transceivers are
heartbeating, you must therefore in this situation be sure
to turn your DELNI's heartbeat OFF.  As you note, if a VMS node
is attached to a transceiver with heartbeat off, it will log
"collision detect failure" errors.  However, this will have no
actual impact on performance, other than the slight additional
overhead caused by the error logging.

Aaron

