In article <323eji$498@hermes.ridgefield.sdr.slb.com>, 
lovell@sdr.slb.com (John Lovell) writes:

>Hi, is anyone out there running OpenGl on Alpha's running VMS?
>I am trying to build some SGI sample programs, with not too much
>success.

There certainly are people running OpenGL on OpenVMS/AXP. The sample
programs available directly from SGI should work, but there may be
some small operating system or hardware dependencies left. The easiest
way to get OpenGL example programs is to install that portion of
Open3D (the software product which contains OpenGL support plus PEX
support). This includes .MMS files and .COM files to help build the
examples.

>Is there documentation I can get to say which libraries to link to,
>for example?

There is not much documentation on OpenGL itself (we reference the
_OpenGL Programming Guide_ (Addison Wesley, ISBN 0-201-63274-8) as
the best way to learn to program in OpenGL), but there is a document
entitled _Using OpenGL_ (order number AA-Q1TDC-TE) that describes
how to compile and link OpenGL programs on both OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1
operating systems. There are also guidelines for writing code that 
runs correctly whether a `long' has 32 or 64 bits, plus some notes about
the implementation on various graphics subsystems.

To answer your immediate question:

              To link OpenGL programs on OpenVMS, create a linker options
              file as follows:

              $ CREATE OPENGL.OPT
              !
              SYS$SHARE:DECW$OPENGLUSHR.EXE/SHARE
              SYS$SHARE:DECW$OPENGLSHR.EXE/SHARE
              !
              SYS$SHARE:DECW$XLIBSHR/SHARE
        <CTRL/Z>

              Use the LINK command line as follows:

              $ LINK TEST, OPENGL.OPT/OPT

If you are using the OpenGL Widget, add 

	      SYS$SHARE:DECW$OPENGLWIDGETSHR.EXE/SHARE

to the options file. By the way, this is also in the release notes.

>How can I find the list of subroutines in a shareable image. I tried
>
>$LIB/LIS/SHARE  DECW$OPENGLSHR.EXE

$ ANALYZE/IMAGE/OUTPUT=T.TMP SYS$LIBRARY:DECW$OPENGLSHR.EXE
$ SEARCH T.TMP "Symbol:"

Perhaps a better way to understand how to use the routines would be
a perusal of SYS$LIBRARY:GL.H. The function prototypes help with
argument numbers and types, sometimes even the use of the
arguments:

	extern void glColor3f (GLfloat red, GLfloat green, GLfloat blue);

is pretty obvious, although:

	extern void glFogf (GLenum pname, GLfloat param);

doesn't really give a lot of information.

>In particular, the SGI routines call a subroutine FFS to identify the
>first bit set in an int. Is this routine in a standard library/include
>file? I see oblique mention of it in PEXMACROS.H. 

That is a UNIX-ism. The version of libtk/getset.c that Digital delivers
as part of the OpenGL example programs contains a definition:


static int ffs (unsigned int mask)
{
    int                 num;
    unsigned int        bit;

    if (mask == 0) return 0;

    for (num = 1, bit = 1; (mask & bit) != 0; num++, bit <<= 1) {}

    return num;

}

>How about Open Inventor. Is there an chance that if Open GL works on the
>Alpha (VMS not OSF) then Open Inventor will work?

Open Inventor is not currently available for sale on OpenVMS; I can't say 
when or if it will be. I suggest you ask the question in comp.graphic.opengl.

>Regards (and sadly confused)

I am sorry that you are confused. If there is something we could be doing
better with our documentation, please let us know.

Andy V (Only a Digital Spoke, not a Digital Spokesperson)


