>In article <11AUG199408483833@almach.caltech.edu>,
>Timothy D. Shoppa x4256 <shoppa@almach.caltech.edu> wrote:

>>Another very excellent group for such discussions is alt.sys.perq.

>Excuse my ignorance, but what exactly is perq? And what's it got to
>do with old DEC hardware?

Basically... a very, very, brief introduction to PERQs:

The PERQ was the first _commercial_ graphics workstation (no, Sun was
not the first commercial workstation, as it has claimed (lied about)
on a CNN news special report), produced by Three Rivers Computer Corp.
(3RCC) in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, which later changed its corporate
name to PERQ Systems Corp.  ICL also manufactured these workstations
under license for distribution in the U.K. and Europe.  When PERQ
Systems went out or business, Varityper began producing PERQs, for a
brief period of time, under its own name; I think it was produced as
the Varityper 1280, but I could be wrong about the model number, but
if anyone wants to know, I can check my notes.

The original PERQs used a proprietary microcoded bit-slice CPU based
on the AMD 2901 chips.  The machine has very fast bit-Blit/RasterOp
graphics (monochrome) on a 1280 x 1024 landscape display (portrait
displays were also available).  Later PERQs, of which even fewer were
produced, had CPUs based on the M68K chips and were sold by Crossfield
Electronics (in England), after PERQ Systems went out of business, as
the Studio 9000, a very high-end color graphics workstation.

Operating systems which ran on the PERQs include: POS (no, it doesn't
mean "point of sale"!, and is also different from DEC's POS), Accent
(the direct predecessor of Mach), PNX (ICL's UNIX for PERQs) and FLEX
(from RSRE).

Probably the reason that Mr. Shoppa mentioned alt.sys.perq was as an
example of a successful newsgroup specific to an older computer
system, to prove that newsgroups for specific types of older
computers, which don't have many users, can succeed and be useful, as
opposed to having one newsgroup to cover all older computers as
someone else suggested.  Basically, all that PERQs have to do with DEC
computers is that the first PERQ had its microcode loaded into it
through a link to a PDP-11.

