In article <5UWSQ2_va5B@netspy.tynet.sub.org>, matt@netspy.tynet.sub.org (Matthias Schulz) writes:
> Does someone know, how I can connect internal drives to this controller.  
> i.e. is there a cable available that maps the 100-pin to 50-pin standard ?  
> Or do I have to use special DEC devices ?
> I have a TZ30 tape drive, but this has a standard 50-pin connector and was  
> put out of a Starage Expansion Box (looks like the desktop Vax/DEC-Station  
> cases.

  What you have is called the "VS3100 SCSI/SCSI controller". It should have
come with a round 50-wire cable formed into a loop, with 4 (I think) 50-pin
connectors and 1 100-pin connector. The 100-pin connector plugs into the con-
troller and you use the others for internal devices. There's also supposed
to be a flat 50-wire cable from the controller to the fromt right drive slot,
for a TZ30 or RX2xS diskette. It is on the "external" SCSI bus.

  The external connector is a 68-pin mini connector. If you look, you'll see
it's longer than a SCSI-2 high density connector and is also the wrong gen-
der.

> Or do I have to make my own calble for this issue ? In this case, is the  
> someone outside knowing the pinout for these 100-pin and 68-pin connectors  

  The external SCSI bus won't work without the 50-pin flat cable inside the
case; the internal bus needs the special round cable as well. If you don't
have these, send me some e-mail and I'll look up the part numbers for you.
If you need the funny terminator that goes on the external connector, or the
part number for an external cable that has a regular 50-pin Amphenol con-
nector on the other end, let me know as well.

  All of these are specialty parts used only on these systems, so you'll need
to get them from DEC (or a reseller) or have them custom-made. Buying from DEC
will probably be less expensive...

