Hi,

I got three harddisks connected to my personal DecStation 5000/33 running 
Ultrix 4.4:

         rz0 : QUANTUM PD1800S       3162,      1.8GB, external
         rz1 : FUJITSU M2694S-512    013E,      1.0GB, external
         rz2 : DEC     RZ25_(C) DEC  0900,      500MB, internal

        and one CD-ROM and a HP-Dat Drive:

         rz4 : DEC     RRD42_(C) DEC 4.5d
         tz5 : HP      HP35470A      9 09
 
Now in order to decide which disk I should use as the main working disk,
I ran the Bonnie harddisk benchmark on my two external disks and got
very peculiar results:

For the Quantum PD1800S I got:

              -------Sequential Output-------- ---Sequential Input-- --Random--
              -Per Char- --Block--- -Rewrite-- -Per Char- --Block--- --Seeks---
Machine    MB K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU  /sec %CPU
DS5000     100   693 95.1  1091 52.0   366 15.8   736 94.8  1350 36.5  37.7  9.6


For the FUJITSU M2694-512 I got:

              -------Sequential Output-------- ---Sequential Input-- --Random--
              -Per Char- --Block--- -Rewrite-- -Per Char- --Block--- --Seeks---
Machine    MB K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU  /sec %CPU
DS5000     100   343 41.8   441 18.5   344 14.2   603 88.1  1439 44.3  45.2 14.0

Both tests were performed in single user mode with no other apps. running and
both results are reproduceable.

Note, that the FUJITSU's output data rate is **HALF** of the data-rate of 
the QUANTUM for both, character and block output.

The CPU load for the FUJITSU is about half of the QUANTUM CPU load as well.

So my conclusion: There must be something wrong with some timing parameters
of the FUJITSU disk.

Question: Is there anything I can do about it? Perhaps change some CAM
parameters or something like that.

I already compared the disk parameters using "rzdisk -g current"
and found that the Quantum has a

               Bus inactivity limit 0

whereas the FUJITSU has a

              Bus inactivity limit 50

This value can not be changed, though.

So please: Anyone with any idea please drop me a line. If there is enough
demand, I'll post a summary of answers, of course.

Thanks and bye now -- Tom

  


