In article <1994Aug10.190632.25036@oxvaxd>, ayoung@vax.oxford.ac.uk (Geordie) writes:
|> I've heard an ugly rumour that the Alpha chip implements floating point
|>division in software rather than hardware.  Naturally the salesman denied
|>it, but the original source of the rumour was a DEC engineer.  Does anyone
|>have more information on this ?

Not true.  Of course, floating divide is not as fast as other instructions
and the compilers have various tricks where they can (if told to or if they
know it's safe) to substitute a multiply by an inverse.

Your source may have been thinking of integer divide, for which there is
no specific instruction.  Compilers implement integer division with a 
low-overhead call to a subroutine which does the divide in other instructions;
it's really quite clever about it and fast. 

The whole point of RISC is that complex operations are often better done as
a series of simpler operations. 
