In <muzic.776365235@ds2.uh.cwru.edu> muzic@ds2.uh.cwru.edu (Ray Muzic) writes:

>I am customizing a public domain code which is mainly fortran (program +
>approx 300 subroutines and functions) but has a collection of c routines
>that do plotting via xwindows.  The fortran code defines a function screen
>and the c code defines an int screen.  When I build this application I get
>an access violation when the c routine tries to assign the value zero to 
>screen.  This makes sense because according to the debugger,
>screen is considered top be a function.  Moreover, when I changed the
>name screen to rscreen in the c code, the program works fine.
>
>My questions are these:
>1) Is this a bug?  Should c and fortan keep their own copies of screen?

Yes, this is a bug. Nobody guarantees separate copies of screen.

>1) Why does this program work correctly on other (Unix) platforms?  Is it
>   because some platforms append underscores, capitalize, or otherwise 
>   modify variable names of fortran variables?

On many Unix platforms, the Fortran screen becomes _screen_ after
compilation and the C screen becomes _screen. No conflict. In Unix,
any program ends up being linked with the C library. But the Fortran
library contains some functions with the same name as functions from
C library. This trick solves the problem. The preceding underscore is
added to avoid conflicts between the user symbols and the symbols used
internally by the C library.

>2) Is there a way to link things differently to solve this problem for VMS?

I don't think so.

Dan
