In article <32au0q$pgu@fnnews.fnal.gov> morphis@D0TAGE.FNAL.GOV writes:
>While the bit widths look reasonable the throughput confuses me,
>the numbers I have seen for PC buses are
>
>bus	width	freq	throughput
>	bits	MHz	Mbytes/s
>ISA	16	 8?	  8
>Eisa	32	 8?	 33
>VLB	32	33?	133
>
>To make the quoted numbers work I would need
>300	64	 6.25	 50
>400	256	0.05	106
>

The PC bus numbers are "speed-of-light", i.e. what is the maximum
throughput of bits that can be observed on the wires, not what
can be actually achieved. Also, they are speeds of I/O buses, whereas
the numbers for the 300 and 400 are the speeds for the memory
system.  Actual throughput of these buses even with large DMA
transfers is about 50-60% of the throughput numbers you quote.

The 300 and 400 numbers are the actually achieved read bandwidth from
the processor (i.e. programmed reads), not some artifical numbers. Your
300 number is also wrong:

	The 300 series reads a cache line in 4 transactions of
	8 bytes each. The latency for the first block is
	200 ns, the remaining three blocks is 80 ns each,
	for a total of 440 ns for 32 bytes or 66 Mbytes/sec.

		Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz

