Lennart Regebro <Lennart.Regebro@scs.no> wrote:
>Is the PDP-10 community really still big enough? Even Stacken computer club in 
>Sweden are more of a Unix place nowadays.

Well, that's of course the question.

I had similar misgivings when creating alt.os.multics. However, that group
turned out to be a resounding success, mostly because there were a lot
of Multicians around who wanted to share their knowledge and spread it
on the net. 

I *hope* alt.sys.pdp10 could be something like that. THe current PDP-10
community may not be very large, but there are lots of people who have
used PDP-10's in the past, and if could just get them started posting
info and reminiscences...

>I think that just alt.sys.pdp-all would be better. Or? 

No!!! There are already alt.sys.pdp8 (which is very active, do check it
out some day) and alt.sys.pdp11. There's no need to try to take over those
groups.

>What I would like is a newsgroup for all kinds of old computers. 
>A.f.c is too big, and could benefit from a split into the two cathegories
>of discussing old systems and discussing hackerdom.
>
>Maybe alt.sys.obsolete? Or would comp.sys.obsolete be better? 

It's a good idea; I've missed such a group, too. However, the name isn't
good - "obsolote" is such a negative word! Maybe comp.sys.classic
or comp.sys.historic? Comp.sys.antique might be overdoing it a bit. :-)

>Alt.folklore.compyters.obsolete has its points too.

Well, where would that leave alt.folklore.computers? Should all posts
dealing with "obsolete" computers move into the new group, while only
posts about modern machines would stay in alt.folklokre.computers?
(So if a discussion about the folkore of Pentium machines turned to
similar stories about the original IBM PC, it should move
over to the "obsolete" group?)

Besides, I don't quite agree with your point about splitting a.f.c along
the lines of "old systems" vs. "hackerdom" - many of the "hackerdom"
posts involve old computers in a very direct way. Where, for example,
would you put discussions about the very PDP-specific MIT hacker culture 
depicted in the (original) Jargon File?

But I do think we could need a group, preferably in the comp
hierarchy, about old computers in general. Such a group could be
dedicated to more technical issues than a.f.c. And of course we could
still have specific groups for PDP-8, PDP-10, and so on. 


