In article: <1994Aug10.190632.25036@oxvaxd>  ayoung@vax.oxford.ac.uk (Geordie) 
writes:
> 
>  I've heard an ugly rumour that the Alpha chip implements floating point
> division in software rather than hardware.  Naturally the salesman denied
> it, but the original source of the rumour was a DEC engineer.  Does anyone
> have more information on this ?
> 

The Alpha has no _integer_ hardware divide; it has four _floating_ divides
(two for VAX-format 32/64-bit numbers, two for IEEE format 32/64-bit numbers).
Note that the two other VAX formats (i.e. H_floating and D_floating) are not 
directly supported by the Alpha hardware.

From the architecture manual:
Alpha hardware supports add, subtract, multiply, divide, convert between
floating formats, convert between floating and integer formats, and compare.
Software routines support square root, remainder, round to integer in floating
point format, and convert binary to/from decimal.

HTH

-- 
Roger Barnett



