>The DECsystem-20 (there was -2020, -2040, -2050, -2060 and -2065 that
>I know of offhand) ran TOPS-20. A slightly different package was called
>a DECSYSTEM-10, and ran TOPS-10.

Correct. The largest of the systems, the 2060 and 2065, were in large
cabinets the size of the VAX-11/780 cabinets, 'cept there were up to 5 or 6
bays rather than just the two used in most 11/780's. And the paint scheme
was burnt orange.

Inside the 2060 and 2065, you would find a PDP-11/40 with a RX02 floppy
drive; this was used to boot the 2060.

>Both were a pdp-10! Not a "modified pdp" :-)
>It's a 36 bit arhcitecture. Memory reference instructions had an
>18-bit address range, but the KL-10B CPU had 23 bit addresses.
>The CPU was not too fast, but you could do I/O on the machine
>like few modern unix boxes.

And the file system was a joy to use comparied to RMS, both to program and
maintain. 

>>The system was TOPS-10, or TOPS-20. This system felt good, with a nice
>>auto-completion feature, a weird file system (with file versions,
>>password-protected directories, recoverable erases, automatic file
>>offlining...). 
>
>That was TOPS-20. Tops-10 was quite different.
>You could run some other OSes on the machine as well.

>There are still sites running pdp-10's, but they are getting fewer
>all the time.

Indeed. I work for cisco Systems and we used to have 4, count 'em (4)
DEC-20's with which the company was started. When I arrived in '91, we had
just pushed two of them out to make room for these tiddly-winks Unix boxes,
but the two remaining machines were still in heavy use, especially by the
hardware engineers. We finally pushed the last one into cold storage about
9 months ago; the rest are gone. The machine which used to run the nic when
it was at SRI was a DEC-20.

Many of the early cisco s/w engineers were pulled from the ranks of
TOPS-10/20 hackers. The h/w engineers used a package called "SUDS"
(Stanford University Design System) for some of our board designs.

In fact, cisco was founded a long time ago not to build routers, but to
build Ethernet interfaces for the DEC-20 machines. You'd have to ask
someone who has been here longer than I how many we sold.

>There is a new company in MA, called XKL, which seems to be building a
>new machine which is pdp-10 compatible. So it's hardly a dead machine
>yet.

Not quite. The company is indeed called XKL, but it is in the Seattle
Washington area, not Massachussets. XKL was started by the founder of
cisco, Len Bosack, who is something of a DEC-20 nut. They're trying to
build a desktop DEC-20 last I heard.

>>I have good memories from this machine, the system was really nice
>>compared to the VMS concurrent (we also had a VAX) and even to the base
>>UNIX (but you can do lots on the top of unix). Nice memories overall.
>
>Still much better OS that either VMS or UNIX.

Agreed. I did some recreational hacking on the machines we had for a couple
of years before we shut 'em down. Very pleasant and easy to use. An
outstandingly rich instruction set if you are an assembly hacker. It makes
it very apparent that DEC picked the wrong operating system as the basis
for VMS -- They should have taken some lessons from TOPS-20 rather than
that filthy abomination called RSX.

dsa




