Well, you jumped a little off, but not too bad, I guess... :-)

>I worked on a Decsystem 20-60 for a while. Architecturally, I would say it looked
>like a modified PDP, with a silly 18 bits adress space (256K) and a fairly slow
>processor (less than 1 mips). The case was kinda big, 5' high maybe 20' long and
>3' width. It booted with 8" diskettes and had a funny wrapping panel like most
>VAXens.

The DECsystem-20 (there was -2020, -2040, -2050, -2060 and -2065 that
I know of offhand) ran TOPS-20. A slightly different package was called
a DECSYSTEM-10, and ran TOPS-10.
Both were a pdp-10! Not a "modified pdp" :-)
It's a 36 bit arhcitecture. Memory reference instructions had an
18-bit address range, but the KL-10B CPU had 23 bit addresses.
The CPU was not too fast, but you could do I/O on the machine
like few modern unix boxes.

>The system was TOPS-10, or TOPS-20. This system felt good, with a nice
>auto-completion feature, a weird file system (with file versions,
>password-protected directories, recoverable erases, automatic file offlining...).

That was TOPS-20. Tops-10 was quite different.
You could run some other OSes on the machine as well.

>When I worked on it, about 1985, we were told DEC would stop maintaining the
>DECsystems, our machine disappeared shortly after. I think they all dissapeared
>before the 90s.

Wrong. DEC stopped hardware development in 1983-84. Software was
developed an additional 5 years, and stopped 1989 or 1990.
We have two -2060 here, both are standing now, because floorspace
is scarce, but they still work. Shut the last one down (hopefully
temporarily) at the end of march this year.
There are still sites running pdp-10's, but they are getting fewer
all the time.

There is a new company in MA, called XKL, which seems to be building a
new machine which is pdp-10 compatible. So it's hardly a dead machine
yet.

>I have good memories from this machine, the system was really nice compared to
>the VMS concurrent (we also had a VAX) and even to the base UNIX (but you can do
>lots on the top of unix). Nice memories overall.

Still much better OS that either VMS or UNIX.

