>Limited View,  Loss Notification,  Output Flow Control,
>Remote Modification,  Security,  Verification

Lee:

I use XOFF instead of CTS control, because I can then use the port for terminal 
access without reconfiguring; I suppose hard control is faster, but I don't 
have anything big enough to matter. At 19200 with 14.4s, most of the control is 
at the sending end, so I don't think it's really much of a slowdown.
Another advantage of XOFF for me is that you know your 
wiring is good if you can talk to the modem, so you don't need a breakout box 
to test it, and it's in the flow, so there are no timing issues.
Just set the flow cntrl to XOFF and disable modem control.

If you set up a service (mine is called MODEM) on the DS, you can use it to 
call out, too. Make sure to set the modem to AT\J0 to fix the serial port 
speed, in case you get a downgraded connection. Autobaud does not work well in 
these cases, you have to retreat to the server and log the port off to reset 
the speed.

Screw the phone co. tip: if your VAX is located on the edge of a local calling 
area, and you have 2 of these setups, you can call in on one modem & then out 
on the other to "leapfrog" local calls, and sometimes save a toll charge. 

