>
>    We currently have a VAX 8200 with 22MB memory, running VMS 5.2.
>I realize the 8200 is pretty ancient (I don't think DEC sells them anymore?),
>so when it gives up the ghost, I'm wondering what kind of upgrade or
>replacement options we might have.  Is the cpu upgradable, keeping the old
>disk and tape drives, memory, etc?  Or is it even worth the cost and effort
>to keep it running, and instead look at a complete replacement unit?

When we were selling it there was an upgrade for the 8200 to a faster
CPU (8250) and the addition of a 2nd CPU to make it a 8300/8350.  The
upgrade path (of the time) from there would have been to a VAX 8530,
8700 or 8800.  They used the same I/O bus as the 8200 used as the
system bus, so all the VAXBI options would have been reusable.  Since
the larger systems only used the VAXBI as an I/O the memory wouldn't
be reusable.  That that was then.

You could upgrade to a member of the VAX 6000 family that supports
the VAXBI for I/O.  That would probably allow reusing the disks, tape
and their controllers.  It also allows for considerably more memory,
more and faster CPUs.  Depending on the model you may have to upgrade
VMS.

A lower cost alternative but one that may not allow reusing as much
old hardware would to be switch to a member of the VAX 4000 family.
The cost difference may be enough to make up for having to buy new
disks and a tape drive.

Once you start looking into software conversion to go to a different
system architecture, you should look at the various AXP systems
available.  You can keep running VMS, but you'll have to migrate
the programs.  This also allows the option of switching to UNIX later
by running OSF/1 on the system.

I'm guessing that the 8200 has a TU81 or TU81+ 9 track tape drive.
If you're really attached to this kind of tape drive, that may
put the most limits on the systems to consider.


