>Short questions: I'm considering to upgrade our personal DecStation 5000/33
>to an R4000 processor
>
>   -- Is that worthwhile? I'm already running Ultrix 4.4!

This is an extremely subjective question.  It depends on a lot of other
factors, as well.  Before I start, I want to try to avoid getting flamed
any more than necessary.  I pretty much like DEC equipment, even after
all the jerking around that DEC customers have been subjected to over
the past couple of years especially Ultrix customers).  That said, here
is my opinion...

First, keep in mind that the DECstation line is pretty much dead; there
will never be any way to milk any more out of it once you upgrade to the
R4000 processor.  Likewise, Ultrix is also dead; there will never be
another major release now that you are running 4.4, there will only be
bug fixes.  I'm afraid that this is going to get inconvenient, especially
in the area of device support, but there is no way to be sure yet.

Second, the R4000 upgrade is relatively expensive.  I don't have a price
list handy, but DEC is certainly not holding a "fire sale" on these.  In
view of the previous observations about the hardware and software, if you
have any possibility at all of completely replacing the machine rather
than upgrading it, you might want to seriously consider using the money
for that.

However, if you have a good reason to hang onto the DECstation and upgrade
it, and there are quite a few good reasons for this, then you are likely
to be pleased with the result.  I had a DECstation 5000/25 at home which I
upgraded to a 5000/50, and the performance difference is very noticable.
One trivial example comes to mind; I do a fair amount of work and testing
with C-Kermit.  Compiling the complete C-Kermit release with the old cpu
took about 8 or 9 minutes; with the R4000 cpu it takes less than 3 minutes.
Absolutely nothing else on my machine was changed, not even the Ultrix
version at the time of the upgrade.  So the entire speed difference is
due to the R4000 upgrade.  I notice the difference throughout my work,
as well.  In my office we have a DECsystem 5100, which is R3000 based
and can not be upgraded.  I very frequently notice how much faster my
system at home is.

jw


