1. It depends on what kind of modem. I know most hayes compatible can be
 configured. If you have a desktop DEC modem it probably has a MMJ connector 
also. You need to run a straight pin to pin 6 wire minimum cable from your 
terminal to the DEC modem. If it is hayes...You'll have to go to a Radio Shack 
andget on of those Telco to RS-232 25 pin adapters and wire it up. If you have 
any of the books for the terminals they should give you an idea how to wire it 
up. Either way anytime you wire a DTE device (terminal) to a DCE device (modem) 
it is always a straight connection. xmit to xmit, rcv to rcv. To test the cable 
simply plug it in to the terminal and short pins 2 and 3 together. Type 
something on the screen. You should see what you type. POWER OFF THE MODEM. 
Connect the cable to the modem...power it up...and see if the TR or DTR lights 
up. Type a few keys and look if you see the SD light flicker. Also check at the 
bottom of the terminal on the status line and see if you see DSR and something 
new not seen there. If not having any luck (if a hayes) connect this modem to a 
PC and type AT&C0&D1&W. This will force the modem to appear to be hardware 
protocol connected to a terminal. Reconnect the terminal and modem as before 
and type ATDT<phone number>. This is one of about three ways of doing this. I 
made some assumptions...If you think this stinks...leave more info on your 
arrangement and I'll be glad to help. Good luck


