>Howdy,
>
>   I am trying to integrate an RZ24L and an RZ26 into my PC using an Adaptec
>1542B interface.  I have been using the RZ24 for quite some time now as my
>boot drive (device 0) without problems.  When I installed the RZ26, I added
>ASPI2DOS.SYS to my config.sys and tried to run (RZ26 = device 1). 

	In addition to checking the termination as someone else suggested,
it would probably help if you used the proper ASPI driver.  The 154x adapters
need ASPI4DOS.SYS, not ASPI2DOS.SYS.  This should have been covered in the
documention that came with the drivers.  Actually, if the RZ24L and RZ26 are
the only things hanging off the SCSI bus and you have no other disks, you
could probably do without the driver.  I forget whether the RZ26 is large
enough to require updated ROMs on the 1542B.  If you still have problems,
you might try asking over in comp.periphs.scsi.

>
>   The first time I booted, the bios saw the drive and added it as D (so far
>so good).  However, when I started fdisk, during its initialization, tried to 
>the drive faulted and stopped responding.
>
>   When I rebooted, the drive faulted before the bios could even find it. I
>discovered that by leaving the system powered off for > 2 minutes, the drive 
>would not fault during the bios init, but as with the first boot I did, would
>fault as soon as I started fdisk.
>
>   Should I assume the drive is bad?  I believe I have the right jumper
>settings - at least I can hear the drive spinning up and it's being found
>at the right address.  I don't have the exact syntax of the DEVICE command
>in config.sys where I load ASPI2DOS.  Can someone post the correct parameters
>for this line?  (the adaptec is at address 330h)  The guy I bought the disk
>from gave me a copy of ASPI2DOS but no documentation.  Would someone be kind
======^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>enough to list a quick summary of the command line options for ASPI2DOS.SYS?

	The ASPI* drivers should have come with your Adaptec card unless you
bought it bare (in which case, penny wise, pound foolish.)  Call Adaptec
and buy a driver kit.  There are various levels for HDs, CDs, tapes, etc.

	Good luck, Sam
