We just took delivery of a pair of Digital 2100 Model 500MP systems
(aka "Sable"), and there's something in the owner's guide that seems
rather peculiar.  Both Digital and the DEC distributor from which we
bought the systems are working to clarify the issue, but as there's
been no answer yet, I'm hoping that someone from the DEC Sable group
might be able to provide an answer.

For reasons not worth boring everybody with, these systems will live
in a room two floors above the ethernet cable to which they'll need
to be attached.  Also, that ethernet is already longer than it should
be, so (unless we shell out some money for a repeater), extending the
ethernet to the Sables isn't a practical solution.

No problem (or so we thought):  All we need are some long (30 metre)
transceiver cables to connect the boxes to a couple transceivers two
floors below.  The folks who sold us the systems knew what we planned
to do.  (In fact, they sold us the transceiver cables!)

Well, the systems arrived last week.  So I started to page through
the user's guide, and in the "Connecting to Networks" section, one
finds:
		----------  NOTE  ----------
		AUI cables cannot extend more
		than twenty meters in length.

Huh?  Digital sells transceiver cables up to *forty* metres long!  So
this can mean one of only two things:

(1)  For some reason, the user's guide is being overly conservative in
its specifications.

(2)  There's something weird with the Sable's ethernet hardware, which
makes it work unreliably if the transceiver cable is more than twenty
metres long.

I'd appreciate it if someone could enlighten me as to whether one can
actually use a transceiver cable longer than 20m.  (And, if so, what's
the reason for the misleading documentation?  And if it really *can't*
be longer than 20m, why are these rather high-end systems built with a
substandard ethernet interface?)

