In article <campo.775856275@calspan.com> campo@calspan.com (Jim Campobello) writes:
>In <CttE9I.JJH@news.tufts.edu> mlevin@jade.tufts.edu (Mike Levin) writes:
>
>>    I have this DEC CD drive - it says RRD40 on the front (but I have
>>no documentation for it). Can someone explain the following to me:
>>1) it seems to have a SCSI port in back. Will it work with
>>   DecStations, VaxStations, or both?
>
>It works with our VAXstation and MicroVAXes SCSI ports.  We don't have
>a Decstation, so I don't know for sure but would guess it should work.

Works with DECstations and Alpha AXP systems too, any reasonably modern
version of VMS or Ultrix should have the software drivers for it.
 
>>2) how, physically, does one load a CD into it? I assume you need some
>>   kind of plastic holder for the CD, but the only one I have seems too tall. 
>
>As you assume, there's a plastic holder that the CD goes into (rather
>awkwardly, but functional).  The holder is about 5"x6".  You insert it
>in the RRD40 -- there are notches and an arrow to indicate which way it
>goes -- and pull it out; the CD stays inside.  You insert the holder again
>to remove the CD.
>
>You can buy the holders from DECdirect, 800-344-4825.  Sorry, I don't
>have the part number.  Last year they were $20 for 5 of them.

Make sure you order the carriers for the RRD40, these are different from
the RRD42 carriers. You probably have a RRD42 style carrier which is a
also used in a lot of PC drives (SONY made the RRD42 mechanism).

>>3) Is there some particular format for CDs that this thing will read?
>>   Will it read standard CDs that exist for IBMs? Is it what is known
>>   as a "double-speed" drive?

Depends entirely on the software you are using it will read any CD-ROM
that has a file system the software knows how to read, essentially
ISO-9660 and Digital's various proprietary formats. It is a single speed
drive.

>>4) Provided it works, how (in terms of software) does one read files
>>   off it? I will be using it from a DecStation running Ultrix, or
>>   barring that, from a VaxStation running VMS. What utilities do I need
>>   to get files off it? Or, do I mount it and use it as a regular file
>>   system or something?
>
>Your last point is correct.  You mount the device and read it as you
>would a disk drive.

Make sure you specify the -r flag to the mount command. Also if it is a
ISO-9660 CD you must add the -t cdfs flags. CDFS support is included in
ULTIRX and you choose to enable or disable it at initial system load,
you can turn it on by editing the kernel configuration if it's not
already on.

