[...]
#> Along comes DLT now, and having been burned by 8mm, I'm going to be very
#> conservative in embracing this technology.  I want to see good bug free
#> drivers, third or fourth generation head technology, multi-sourced supplies
#> like cleaning kits and certified tapes.  SUN, HP, IBM, and yes, even DEC
#> will need to cast their votes in favor of the technology by making it a
#> "must have", like CD's and (apparently) 4mm DAT.  I don't see this type
#> of market acceptance yet, so I'll continue to tough it out with 8mm, and
#> hope that the competition from 4mm and DLT kicks Exabyte in the pants enough
#> to "open" them up and to further evolve the medium.
#>  
#> JR Bull
#> State of Oreton
#> Water Resources Department
#
#
#I understand your reservations about "new" technology and the potential for
#problems. DLT is no longer that new, the TA85 has been around for a few years
#now, its predecessors (TK50 and TK70) quite a bit longer.
#
#The technology attracted me initially because of its speed over exabyte. We
#also soon noticed its far superior reliability. I also like the backwards
#compatibility with earlier versions. There's none of the problems associated
#with compression algorithms that you get with both 4mm and 8mm. 

Yeah, but the Tx87 is higher density than the Tx86 than the Tx85, and a low-
density drive can't read a high-density tape (of course).  Similar problem, but
at least any Tx87 drive can read any other Tx87 drive's tapes.

#When I hooked an SA100 (TA85 with stacker) to a VAX6610/HSC70 cluster I had
#no problems at all. On another cluster with a TZ86 connected to an HSC95 with
#K/SCSI card there were some initial firmware problems. Once resolved we've
#seen reliability go up and our backup times reduced by a third.

At my last job, we moved from two 8500s to (more disks and) two TA867 stackers.
Big improvements -- the stackers work great (unfortunately, the version of HSC
software we were running when I left isn't compatible with Mickey's MLU utility
to switch loaded tapes and query tapes remotel - must-have if you don't have
the tape drives next to your terminal -- available from ftp.spc.edu in
[.macro32.savesets]) most of the time (only about a jam every other month or so
-- with daily usage.  And the speed, even through the same HSC that was reading
the disk being backed up, was pretty good (much, much better than our
BI->SCSI->8mm -- less than 1/2 the time, if I recall.), and we never had a bad
tape, never a tape we couldn't write to or read from.  They cost a lot more
than 8mm tape, but you get a lot of passes from them.  Only drawback is they
don't fit in your camcorder when you want to retire them!  :-) 

-Dan Wing, wing@tgv.com


