HeeeellllP!  Sorry for a huge post, but I wanted to include the relevent
error log entries.

BACKUP is doing *something* to my MAXTOR 1240S that is completely hosing up
the drive and/or VMS 6.1 on my VS4000/90A.

I can provoke the condition at will using:
   $ BACKUP $3$DKA200:[*...]*.*.* nla0:asdf.bck/sav/log

The symptoms are that the system gums up solid, and if I ^Y the backup fast
enough, I can get back to DCL and do a show dev.  This indicates:
      $3$DKA0:    87 errors		<example>
      $3$DKA200:  258 errors		<example>
and the system is tremendously sluggish, each operation keeps adding errors
to the two drives.  I've listed some of the significant errors below... the
rest are _very_ similar.

If I backup to a slower device than NL... eg. a tlz04, backup gets a little
further and starts spewing errors:
  Virtual file read error for [SYS0.SYSEXE]SYSLOAxxx, Block 53
  Virtual file read error for !AS, Block !UL
                                      <garbage here>|
  Virtual file read error for [SYS0.SYSEXE]SYSLOAxxx, Block 54
  Virtual file read error for !AS, Block !UL
                                      <garbage here>|
etc.

If I let the backup continue, the system will likely crash with a
page read error.  I've been rebooting instead of letting it crash.

I've accessed the drive from a 5.5-2 box via MSCP using the same backup
command.  No problem.  So I inserted a home-brewed disk driver between DKA200
and backup.  Again no problem.  [Thereby I managed to get my backup! :)]

It appears backup is doing something to the drive that the drive doesn't like
& then the drive goes banannas.  Also, a reboot of the box doesn't fix the
drive... I have to power-cycle it to get it to be seen by sysgen autoconfigure.

By way of further testing, I've spawned a couple of DIR/FULL/OUT=NL: [*...]
commands on it simultaneously from two different processes... then do a
monitor sys/int=1 ... I've seen it pumping 1400+ DIOs, NO ERRORS!  Same with
COPY [*...]*.*.* nl:

Has anyone seen anything like this?  Is there any way to fix it?  What further
information should I be collecting/supplying?

All advice & input would be appreciated.  Email is especially appreciated
in the interest of speed.
-- 
