Ah... The joys of 9-pin serial standards.... 

If the 9-pin VT220 printer port is anything like the serial ports on my MINCs,
then it's what I call compressed-25 :-)
You see, there are really only 9 wires of the 25 pins on a _real_ RS232 port
that are needed for single-channel working, which are

pins 1-8 and pin 20 

(or, some people ignore protective ground (1), and include Ring Indicator (22),
but I'm pretty sure DEC used the first set). 

So, what was done was to map pins 1-8 to pins 1-8 of the 9-pin D connector, and
pin 20 to pin 9. Hence my name for it.

Then IBM came and ruined it....

But anyway, you might try mapping the pins like that. 

1 - prot ground
2 - TxD 
3 - RxD
4 - RTS
5 - CTS
6 - DSR
7 - Sig Ground
8 - CD
9 - DTR


