<d_braden@delphi.com> writes:
 
>I suggest you dump 8mm and go for DLT.  8mm isn't a serious backup medium.
 
Oh yeah?  What is the current installed base for DLT?  There are many of us
out here who've been burned by adopting a "hot new" removeable storage media
(yes, 8mm included) only to find that:
1. OS support for our hot new media type was lacking because the OS vendor
   didn't consider the installed base significantly large enough to bother
   with (i.e. Ultrix and its lack of support for Exabyte multidensity /
   non-compression/compression tape drives)
 
2. The first generation (and second generation) hardware product has a
   MTBF of 50,000 hourse, but the one you received from the manufacturer
   ***MUST*** have been in operation for 49,970 hours because it fails
   the first day/week/month that you try to use it.
 
3. By some odd coincidence, the only "certified" media (or cleaning kit,
   or ___<fill in the blank with your favorite must have supply>____, is
   is only manufactured by and available from the outfit that makes the
   tape drive subsystem!
 
I'm sure that there are many of us that belong to the Exabyte club because
we shot our wad on Exabyte technology before 4mm was ready for prime time
(not that the 8200 was!), and have had to stick it out.  Now, 8mm has
matured, and ther error rates aren't so bad with the 8505, and there are
tapes other than Exabyte that work okay. (In my original post on this thread
I stated that I was using Verbatim - WRONG, Maxell).  The cleaning supply
issue is still there though (that seems to be at the core of the original
problem), and if Exabyte were to go belly up we'd all be SOL for parts.
Along comes DLT now, and having been burned by 8mm, I'm going to be very
conservative in embracing this technology.  I want to see good bug free
drivers, third or fourth generation head technology, multi-sourced supplies
like cleaning kits and certified tapes.  SUN, HP, IBM, and yes, even DEC
will need to cast their votes in favor of the technology by making it a
"must have", like CD's and (apparently) 4mm DAT.  I don't see this type
of market acceptance yet, so I'll continue to tough it out with 8mm, and
hope that the competition from 4mm and DLT kicks Exabyte in the pants enough
to "open" them up and to further evolve the medium.
 
JR Bull
